Both routes are IDOR-vulnerable: any authenticated user can cancel or edit/delete any account/order by guessing an id, since the `WHERE` clause only matches `id` and never checks `req.user.accountId`.

Fix: add the account id to the `WHERE` clause (and to the `RETURNING` check) so the update can only ever touch a row owned by the caller.

**`routes/orders.js`** (`/:id/cancel`):
```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

**`routes/accounts.js`** (`PATCH /:id` and `DELETE /:id`): since accounts *are* the tenant here, scope by `id = $n AND id = req.user.accountId` (i.e. a user can only patch/delete their own account, not just any account row):
```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```
(If accounts can ever be admin-managed across tenants, that's a separate authorized path — not this one.)

Also check `db.write` returns `null`/`undefined` on zero rows (vs. throwing) — if it doesn't, the 404 check needs adjusting to whatever shape it actually returns. And do the same audit for any other `:id`-keyed routes in this repo (e.g. GET-by-id, sub-resources) — the same class of bug is likely to repeat wherever `req.params.id` is trusted alone.

